<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="eigen-intro-complete">
    <title>Introduction to Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Given a linear operator <m>A</m>, one can form the <term>eigen-problem</term> for <m>A</m>:
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        for what non-zero vectors <m>\xvec</m> and scalars <m>\lambda</m> is it true that 
                        <m>A \xvec = \lambda \xvec</m>?
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        This problem is very relevant in quantum mechanics, among many other places.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Connection to Quantum Mechanics</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                Recall, Postulate 3 says
                </p>
                <p>
                    In any measurement of the observable associated with the operator <m>\hat{A}</m>, the only values 
                                    that will every be observed are the eigenvalues <m>a_n</m>, which satisfy the eigenvalue equation
                                    <md>
                                        \hat{A}\psi_n = a_n\psi_n
                                    </md>.

                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Using the Hamiltonian operator, this yields,
                        <md>
                            \hat{H} [\psi] = E \psi
                        </md>,
                        which is exactly the time-independent version of Schrödinger's equation!
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        We'll start with some examples from finite dimensional vector spaces, and eventually look at 
                        examples of observables in quantum mechanics (position, momentum).
                    </p>
                </solution>
                
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise>
        <title>Visualizing Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Recalling we can view a linear operator <m>A</m> as an <m>n \times n</m> matrix, what is the intuition behind
                <m>A \xvec = \lambda \xvec</m>? What are the eigenvalues/eigenvectors of 
            </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    2 \amp 0 \\
                    0 \amp 1
                    \end{bmatrix}
                    </m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    You can use the <url href="https://understandinglinearalgebra.org/jsactivities/eigenvectors.html">eigenvector interactive</url> 
                    to visualize that the eigenvalues are <m>2</m> (with eigenvectors of the form <m>\begin{bmatrix} c \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}</m>) and 
                    <m>1</m> (with eigenvectors of the form <m>\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ c \end{bmatrix}</m>).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

         <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    2 \amp 0 \\
                    0 \amp 2
                    \end{bmatrix}
                    </m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    You can use the <url href="https://understandinglinearalgebra.org/jsactivities/eigenvectors.html">eigenvector interactive</url> 
                    to visualize that the only eigenvalue is <m>2</m> (with eigenvectors all of <m>\R^2</m>).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
         <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    0 \amp -1 \\
                    1 \amp 0
                    \end{bmatrix}
                    </m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    You can use the <url href="https://understandinglinearalgebra.org/jsactivities/eigenvectors.html">eigenvector interactive</url> 
                    to visualize that there are no eigenvalues and eigenvectors of this transformation.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

         <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>
                    \begin{bmatrix}
                    1 \amp 2 \\
                    2 \amp 1
                    \end{bmatrix}
                    </m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    You can use the <url href="https://understandinglinearalgebra.org/jsactivities/eigenvectors.html">eigenvector interactive</url> 
                    to visualize that the eigenvalues are <m>3</m> (with eigenvectors of the form <m>\begin{bmatrix} c \\ c \end{bmatrix}</m>) and 
                    <m>-1</m> (with eigenvectors of the form <m>\begin{bmatrix} -c \\ c \end{bmatrix}</m>).
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
            
        </exercise>
</page>

<page>
    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <title>Eigenvectors and Bases</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Eigenvalues and eigenvectors are useful computationally.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The big idea is that eigenvectors associated to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent vectors.
                If a matrix <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors, we can use them to form a basis for the 
                vector space, which can give a useful interpretation of multiplication by <m>A</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>Example: Eigenvectors, Bases and Diagonalization</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We've already seen that the eigenvalues are <m>3</m> (with eigenvectors of the form <m>\begin{bmatrix} c \\ c \end{bmatrix}</m>) and 
                    <m>-1</m> (with eigenvectors of the form <m>\begin{bmatrix} -c \\ c \end{bmatrix}</m>). So we can take the 
                    particular eigenvectors <m>\vvec_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> and <m>\vvec_2 = \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}</m> to 
                    form a basis for <m>\R^2</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Then if <m>\xvec \in \R^2</m>, we can write <m>\xvec = c_1\vvec_1 + c_2 \vvec_2</m> for some scalars <m>c_1, c_2</m>, and 
                so
                <md>
                    <mrow>A \xvec \amp= A (c_1\vvec_1 + c_2 \vvec_2) </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp= c_1 (A\vvec_1) + c_2 (A\vvec_2)</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp= (3c_1)\vvec_1 - c_2 \vvec_2 </mrow>
                </md>
                That is, multiplication by <m>A</m> in this new basis is just scalar multiplication in the directions of
                <m>\vvec_1</m> and <m>\vvec_2</m>!
                
            </p>
            <p>
                More formally, we can decompose <m>A</m> as 
                <md>
                    A = PDP^{-1}
                </md>,
                where <m>D = \begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -1\end{bmatrix}</m> is the diagonal matrix of eigenvalues, and
                <m>P = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}</m> is the matrix whose columns are the associated eigenvectors.
                

            </p>
        </solution>
        
    </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title>Exercise: Diagonalization</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the interactive to diagonalize 
                <md>
                    A = \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp -2 \\ -2 \amp -1 \end{bmatrix}
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>A =  \begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix} 3 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp -2 \end{bmatrix} \left(\begin{bmatrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{bmatrix}\right)^{-1}</m>
            </p>
        </solution>
        
    </exercise>
</page>



       
    </worksheet>
